I think you didn't pay enough attention to Bruce's
objection.

The logical conclusion of your chosen path is that _nothing_
should stack, because stacking is a giveaway that items have
the same b/u/c and +s status.  Every separate dagger picked
up would have to be identified separately, and your comment
earlier that the basic challenge of NetHack is IDing stuff
would change character to become "the thing that makes
NetHack unplayable is all the time you waste IDing stuff".

"Once and for all (modulo amnesia and mind-sucking)
identification" is a playability issue, and NHINRL,
sometimes realism really does need to give way to
playability.

Flat chance undetectable cursing founders on the same issue:
you'd have to completely rewrite the UI be able to stack
stuff with different b/u/c and +s status, or the cursing
would become obvious because the stack got split, and
because otherwise, cursing would have the unintended side
effect of decreasing the PC's overall carrying capacity by
eating slots.

Cursing slotwise is _necessary_ to the UI, in a profound
sense. Another way of characterizing NetHack is as a game
where the _player_, not the PC, learns over time how to
improve the odds of winning.  If that includes learning how
to manage your inventory slots to minimize (note: never
_remove_) the chance of a curse causing overwhelming harm,
well, that is an "education", it isn't an "abuse", any more
than searching for better armor. It is just one of the
things the player learns, like the former balance between
obsessive searching for traps, and dying of starvation
[which got seriously impaired as a part of the game when
#pray-ing came to replace finding food, since a "search"
contributes as much time to your prayer timeout as does a
"move", removing the penalty for obsessive searching;
YANI: only turns spent _moving_ should decrease _some_
timeouts, a profound change to the game].

That we have widely publicized "spoilers" to let players
bypass the in-game learning of these lessons is where the
"abuse" comes into play, and to me, these really do "spoil"
the hard work of the DevTeam in placing devious and
mysterious challenges into NetHack, every bit as much as
any backdoor "cheat code" for a commercial game.  I'm no
innocent here, I published a complete map of Bard's Tale I
for _glory_, never considering how many people's fun in
doing their own learning I was spoiling.

I'm older now, and occasionally have managed to become
stupid about different things, to my credit, I think.

